[Music]  Thomas Jefferson is revered as America's  third president and its first Secretary  of State  he's also the main author of the  Declaration of Independence and one of  four presidents on Mount Rushmore what's  less known is his role as an architect  to help shape the look of early America  an exhibition at the Chrysler Museum of  Art in Norfolk Virginia is exploring  that old and little-known legacy in a  very modern way  brook silva-braga is back from a visit  there Brooke good morning good morning  guys Thomas Jefferson was many things a  politician and a diplomat a naturalist  and a scientist but it's his work as an  architect that's getting special  attention at the moment because it  illustrates both the brilliance of his  vision and what too many is an  unforgivable blind spot just the title  of the show will surprise some of us  Thomas Jefferson architects yes I'm not  sure I even knew that or I forgot it he  was an architect not a professional but  one of the most advanced architectural  thinkers of his time Eric Neal  the curator and museum director says  evidence of Jefferson's influence this  is the Virginia Capitol yes is how  familiar his designs now look so this  looks almost like a cliche of a  government building now it wasn't then  Jefferson was saying we want to do like  what the ancient Romans and what the  ancient Greeks did because they had the  highest form of government and the  legislators acted with wisdom and the  architecture should convey that  Jefferson's plantation home Monticello  carried those principles  this was the drawing that Jefferson  submitted so did his anonymous  submission to a design competition for  the White House this dome was to have  glass windows  it was the most innovative it was the  most advanced well even today this looks  almost futuristic yes I'm not sure could  actually have been built that may be why  it looks like something I haven't seen  these models first appeared at an  Italian retrospective on Jefferson's  architecture Neel saw that show and  wanted to bring it home to Virginia but  realized that was a complicated idea  would it be going too far to say that  you thought that exhibition wouldn't be  well received here it wouldn't be enough  and if I had presented that exhibition I  would have rightly been criticized  Jefferson's designs after all were made  into buildings by slaves yes as a  politician he helped end the Atlantic  slave trade but at Monticello and  elsewhere Jefferson owned more than six  hundred human beings including his own  mistress Sally Hemings do we know who  made this in some cases we do in some  cases we don't so Neil enlisted a  diverse group of advisors to make a new  kind of exhibit it's a very fine work of  art highlighting the otherwise anonymous  people who built things like this door  they've been able to analyze the grain  and the grooves and the details of the  door and connect that to a set of tools  that are known to have been John Hemings  John Hemings was Sally Hemings brother  we don't know who the fingerprints in  this brick belonged to but here it is  under glass next to a handful of  handmade nails and a tribute to Isaac  Granger one of the men who made  Jefferson's nails he's wearing his  workman's apron and his shirt he's  showing you his hands which are signs of  his creativity love it or hate it the  exhibit is a profoundly different way to  present Jefferson I could see people  saying this is an American Hero why are  you trying to bring him down I could see  people saying this is a slaveholder why  are you still building him up I've  gotten a couple letters how could you do  this to Thomas Jefferson why would you  tarnish the image of this great man and  I've had other people say really we want  to give praise to this man who really  had a concubine who had a slave mistress  the reassessment of Jefferson the  architect will continue this spring at  the University of Virginia a campus he  designed  when UVA dedicates this memorial to  enslaved laborers the enslaved worked  below in kitchens that were poorly lit  poorly ventilated Columbia University  professor Mabel Wilson worked on both  the UVA Memorial and the Chrysler  exhibit she studies architecture and  race and says one of Jefferson's  hallmarks was finding ways to hide the  places where slaves lived and worked I  think this is what makes him an  architect that he understands  that the buildings make relationships  between people so he is very shrewd at  using architecture to make invisible  something that he completely understood  was morally reprehensible and against  the values of freedom and equality this  is all part of a larger reckoning in  Virginia for so long we've only told one  side of the story  Janice Underwood is Virginia's  first-ever director of diversity equity  and inclusion hired by governor Ralph  Northam after a racist photo was found  on North homes yearbook page  Virginia's second governor was Thomas  Jefferson who designed the Capitol  building where Underwood now works  he said that all men are created equal  and while I know that to be true he  would not have included me in that he  would not have included Sally Hemings in  that or any of his children with Sally  Hemings Underwood says she isn't sure if  the statue of Jefferson just behind the  Capitol should even stay up so I don't  know we're reckoning with those  questions now in its way the Chrysler  museum is attempting answers to the  questions of Jefferson by teaching new  names and attaching new meaning to old  ones do you want them to come away with  the idea that Thomas Jefferson was a  great American or maybe just an  important American I would say he's a  great American I would say the idea that  we have a president who has ideals that  we still aspire to it's really something  I want people to take away with them to  recognize he fell short of his  aspirations I hope they come away with  that as well  a handful of other museums across the  country are making a similar shift in  some cases actually changing the labels  next to paintings to explain that the  person in the portrait was involved in  the slave trade an important point to  make a great American he may have been  but a flawed one if you hold regard to  the writings of the man himself all men  are created equal it's it's the contrast  or even the hypocrisy at the center of  this exhibition and a lot of the  re-examination of Jefferson is so  important to understand  the past the full story or even news is  a great story  you

